 2. Navigation Architecture

 2.1 Layered Navigators


App.js
└─ SafeAreaProvider
    └─ AuthProvider              (context)
        └─ CartProvider          (context)
            └─ AppNavigator
                └─ NavigationContainer
                    ├─ AuthStack        (when userToken === null)
                    └─ DrawerNavigator  (when userToken !== null)
                        └─ Drawer.Screen "MainTabs"
                            └─ MainTabs (bottom tabs)
                                ├─ Tab.Screen "Home"     → HomeStack
                                │   ├─ HomeMain
                                │   ├─ Products
                                │   ├─ ProductDetails
                                │   └─ Cart
                                ├─ Tab.Screen "Profile"
                                └─ Tab.Screen "Settings"
                            └─ Drawer.Screen "Help"


 2.2 Rule of Thumb

| Navigator | Purpose | When to use |
|--||-|
| Stack (`createNativeStackNavigator`) | Push/pop screens with back button | Home → Products → Details, or any flow that needs back navigation |
| Tab (`createBottomTabNavigator`) | Peer screens, no back stack | Home / Profile / Settings bottom bar |
| Drawer (`createDrawerNavigator`) | Global navigation, side menu | Top-level sections |
| Auth Stack | Login flow | Shown only when not authenticated |

Key insight: Each navigator only manages its direct children. A tab navigator has no back stack — that's why we nested a `HomeStack` inside the Home tab so `Products` and `ProductDetails` can be pushed with a back button.

 2.3 Conditional Root Navigation

jsx
// AppNavigator.js
{userToken !== null ? <DrawerNavigator /> : <AuthStack />}


The root navigator swaps the entire tree based on auth state. When the user logs in, `userToken` updates, and React Navigation automatically replaces the stack — no manual `navigation.navigate('Home')` needed.



 3. Context — Two Providers

 3.1 `AuthContext`

Owns: `userToken`, `isLoading`, `login()`, `logout()`.

Consumed by:
- `AppNavigator` — decides which navigator to render
- `DrawerNavigator` — logout button in custom drawer content
- `ProfileScreen` / `SettingsScreen` — logout action
- `CustomButton` (via `loading={isLoading}`)

Pattern:

jsx
const { logout, isLoading } = useContext(AuthContext);


 3.2 `CartContext`

Owns:
- `items` — array of `{ ...product, qty }`
- `addToCart(product, qty = 1)`
- `removeFromCart(id)`
- `increment(id)` / `decrement(id)` / `updateQty(id, qty)`
- `clearCart()`
- Derived: `totalItems`, `subtotal`, `savings` (via `useMemo`)

Consumed by:
- `HomeScreen` — cart badge count + Add to Cart buttons
- `ProductsScreen` — Add to Cart buttons
- `ProductDetailsScreen` — Add to Cart / Buy Now
- `CartScreen` — full cart UI
- `ProfileScreen` / `SettingsScreen` — clear cart on logout

 3.3 Provider Nesting Rules

jsx
<SafeAreaProvider>       {/ native safe-area context — outermost /}
  <AuthProvider>         {/ auth decides app shell /}
    <CartProvider>       {/ cart depends on auth (clear on logout) /}
      <AppNavigator />   {/ NavigationContainer lives here /}
    </CartProvider>
  </AuthProvider>
</SafeAreaProvider>


| Rule | Why |
||--|
| `SafeAreaProvider` outermost | Required by `react-native-safe-area-context` for insets |
| `AuthProvider` above `CartProvider` | Lets `CartProvider` read auth to clear cart on logout |
| `CartProvider` above `AppNavigator` | Every screen (Home, Products, Cart) can call `useCart()` |
| Never put a provider inside `NavigationContainer` twice | Would create duplicate state — screens see different instances |

 3.4 Custom Hook Pattern

jsx
export const useCart = () => {
  const ctx = useContext(CartContext);
  if (!ctx) throw new Error('useCart must be used within CartProvider');
  return ctx;
};


Why: Fail fast if a component forgets the provider. Better than a runtime `undefined is not an object` deep in the render tree.



 4. Reusable UI Components

 4.1 `CustomText`

Location: `src/components/common/CustomText.js`

Why it exists: Centralizes typography. Every text in the app goes through it, so font family, size scale, and color tokens are consistent.

API:

jsx
<CustomText
  variant="semibold"        // light | regular | medium | semibold | bold
  size={sizes.sm}           // numeric from theme
  color={colors.textPrimary}
  align="left"              // left | center | right
  numberOfLines={2}
  style={...}
>
  {children}
</CustomText>


Key implementation detail: `includeFontPadding: false` + `textAlignVertical: 'center'` for consistent cross-platform vertical alignment.

 4.2 `CustomButton`

Location: `src/components/common/CustomButton.js`

Features:
- Variants: `primary` | `secondary` | `outline`
- Sizes: `sm` | `md` | `lg`
- `loading` state shows `ActivityIndicator` inline
- `disabled` state
- `icon` (Ionicons name) + `iconPosition` (`left` | `right`)
- `fullWidth` flag

Pitfall we fixed: Originally the import `Icon` was shadowed by a prop named `Icon`. Renamed the import to `Ionicons` and the prop to lowercase `icon`.

 4.3 Reusable Row/Section Pattern

Used in `ProfileScreen` and `SettingsScreen`:

jsx
const SettingsRow = ({ icon, label, value, onPress, rightElement, danger }) => (...)
const SettingsSection = ({ title, children }) => (...)
const Divider = () => (...)


Why: A single `MenuRow`/`SettingsRow` component handles 30+ rows across screens. Adding a new settings option is a 5-line change.

Row anatomy:


[icon-circle]  [label text]  [value / Switch / chevron]


Why icon gets a tinted circle: `iconColor + '18'` gives 10% opacity background. It's the modern settings UI look (used in iOS 15+, Google apps, Spotify).

 4.4 Barrel Exports

`src/components/common/index.js`:

jsx
export { CustomText } from './CustomText';
export { CustomButton } from './CustomButton';


Then import as:

jsx
import { CustomButton, CustomText } from '../../components/common';


Why: Shorter imports, one place to add new shared components.



 5. Theme System

 5.1 Files

| File | Contents |
||-|
| `colors.js` | Brand + semantic colors (`primary`, `textPrimary`, `border`, `success`, etc.) |
| `fonts.js` | Font family map: `fonts.family.regular`, `.medium`, `.semibold`, `.bold` |
| `sizes.js` | Numeric scale: `sizes.xs`, `.sm`, `.md`, `.lg`, `.xl`, `.xxl`, plus `radiusSm/Md/Lg` |
| `spacing.js` | `spacing.xs`, `.sm`, `.md`, `.lg`, `.xl` — the only allowed margin/padding values |
| `index.js` | Re-exports everything as `{ colors, fonts, sizes, spacing }` |

 5.2 Rules

- Never hardcode hex colors in screens — always `colors.something`
- Never hardcode padding/margin numbers — always `spacing.something`
- Never hardcode font sizes — always `sizes.something`
- When a semantic color is missing (`colors.white`, `colors.danger`), add an alias to the theme rather than replacing each usage — one change fixes all references

 5.3 Common Pitfall: Missing Color Keys

If `colors.white` is `undefined`, then `color={colors.white}` silently falls back to a default. This caused invisible text on colored backgrounds.

Fix: Add aliases to the theme:

jsx
white: 'FFFFFF',           // alias for colors.textWhite
danger: 'FF3B30',          // alias for colors.error


Or replace every `colors.white` → `colors.textWhite`. Pick one and be consistent.



 6. Common Screen Patterns

 6.1 Screen Component Signature

jsx
export const HomeScreen = ({ navigation }) => { ... }


React Navigation injects `navigation` and `route` into every screen component. Use `navigation.navigate('RouteName', { params })` to move between screens.

 6.2 Reading Route Params

jsx
// On the sender side
navigation.navigate('ProductDetails', { product: item });

// On the receiver side (ProductDetailsScreen)
const { product } = route.params;


 6.3 Reusable Render Functions

Screens define `renderProduct`, `renderCategory`, `renderOffer` as local functions and pass them to `FlatList`'s `renderItem`:

jsx
<FlatList data={products} renderItem={renderProduct} keyExtractor={i => i.id} />


Benefits:
- Keeps the main JSX readable
- Easy to extract into a separate `ProductCard` component later

 6.4 Fixed-Height Chips

jsx
chip: {
  height: 36,                // fixed
  borderRadius: 18,          // = height / 2
  paddingHorizontal: spacing.md,
  alignItems: 'center',      // vertical centering
  justifyContent: 'center',  // horizontal centering
  // NO paddingVertical — height replaces it
}


Why: Using `paddingVertical` alone causes chips to grow/shrink based on content. Fixed height keeps all chips uniform.

 6.5 Tap Priority — Card vs. Inner Button

When a card is `TouchableOpacity` and contains an inner `CustomButton`, the inner button's `onPress` fires first, then the outer card's. On Android, both may fire. Watch for this with Add to Cart buttons inside tappable product cards — use `e.stopPropagation()` if needed.



